State the null model and limits of an exact probability comparison (@inquiry-evidence-limits: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-complete-and-paired-allocations-with-exact-finite-evidence.lingua#r_025C0CC5AAE41742537448C79B") { r_0ACF4290939593619C55D72DC9
A randomization comparison asks how unusual a declared statistic would be under a specified no-effect model and the assignment mechanism. It does not calculate the probability that a scientific hypothesis is true. Begin with the target and effect in units, then state a null model, the allowed assignments, their probabilities, the statistic and which outcomes count as at least as extreme. Choose these before examining the result; changing the tail after seeing a favorable direction changes the procedure.

Use a sharp no-effect null: for every unit, Y_i(Q)=Y_i(P). This is stronger than saying the six effects average to zero. Under the sharp null, each observed indication supplies that unit's indication under either protocol, so the vector of observed values can be held fixed while assignments are changed. Under a null allowing nonzero effects that merely cancel on average, those unobserved alternatives are not supplied by the observed vector, and this same finite calculation is not automatically valid.

For the complete-design example, hold the observed vector (2,4,5,8,10,11) mm fixed. For each of the twenty assignments, calculate Q mean minus P mean. Choose the two-sided extremeness rule |D|≥|D_observed|, including ties. The observed contrast is 6 mm. Two assignments reach absolute contrast 6 mm, so the exact tail probability is 2/20=1/10. This is a conditional calculation under the sharp null and uniform complete allocation, not an estimate of the probability that the true effect is zero. It also does not establish that a 6 mm difference is useful, harmless or accurate.

There is a useful finite validity argument. List the twenty assignments in decreasing absolute statistic. Every tied group receives a tail count that includes that entire group and every more extreme group. For a predeclared threshold α, reject only when that count divided by twenty is at most α. The rejecting groups together occupy at most 20α positions. Under the null's uniform allocation, rejection probability is therefore at most α. Ties and discrete steps can make it smaller. This reasoning requires the correct fixed outcome distribution under the null; it is not obtained simply by repeatedly rearranging any observational dataset.

Complementary balanced assignments reverse D and preserve |D|. Thus every two-sided tail contains at least two assignments, and the smallest possible probability is 2/20=0.1. With this design and statistic, a 0.05 rule cannot reject, whatever the six fixed outcomes. Failing to reject would consequently be very weak evidence for no effect. A one-sided rule has a different question and resolution; selecting it only after seeing the sign does not preserve the predeclared two-sided procedure.

Use the assignment set that was actually justified. The paired design has eight outcomes and excludes the observed assignment (3,4,5), so it cannot be substituted as the reference set for this complete-design realization. If the largest units were purposefully selected for Q, neither uniform reference set describes that allocation mechanism. Randomization inference also does not repair selective outcome reporting, unexplained missing data, post-result exclusions or violations of the fixed potential-response assumptions.

**Read and defend:** [Measurement Process Characterization — NIST/SEMATECH](../metrology/sources/Statistical-Handbook.lingua#r_6S3221KSX1KKGPJNXKC3N022NN), section 7.1.3.1 on critical values and p-values, with the finite-discreteness qualification in its inspection note. Explain the null and tail in your own words. Report the indicated-length contrast, target, allocation, exact tail and limits together. Prove the tie-group count argument for another small outcome set. Distinguish a statistical threshold from a scientific or engineering decision; reuse [Connect an assembly prediction to a bounded inspection decision](../../engineering/core/Connect-an-assembly-prediction-to-a-bounded-inspection-decision.lingua#r_0AE23276AAB728BEDBFC0AC47E) to show that a hard-bound conformance rule answers a different question.

**Continue:** [Investigate complete and paired allocations with exact finite evidence](Investigate-complete-and-paired-allocations-with-exact-finite-evidence.lingua#r_025C0CC5AAE41742537448C79B).

